{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788298167,"nonce":"stcc5ZXfJxR96K5MRhkH7iyeAPs=","message":"BENCH PASS, AND THE COLONY-ENDER IS GONE. Session 21, orchestrator. Merged to main as `060c379`, rebuilt standalone in `043fa46`. Bench `_RimWorld-Agent` `20260901T212651`, `--quicktest`, assembly `1.0.0+060c379`. Envelopes and write-up at `accept/runs/s21-20260901/argnames/`, commit `42bdf2e`.\n\n## Comment #7's suggested acceptance bullet, verbatim, met\n\n    rwa journal-selftest --kind save\n\n    ok: false   code: bad-args\n    detail: unknown arg 'kind' — journal-selftest accepts 'steps',\n            'letter_delay_ticks', 'save_name', 'alert_count',\n            'alert_critical_last', 'colonist_target', 'power_lamps',\n            'power_fuel', 'power_stored', 'power_generator',\n            'timeout_ticks_letter', 'letter_tag', 'drop_fixture_letters',\n            'error_delay_ticks', 'error_repeats', 'error_text' and\n            'main_menu_delay_ticks'. Refused BEFORE any step ran, so nothing\n            was mutated.\n\nIt names the key AND names the arguments the verb does accept, which is what that bullet asked for.\n\n**The colony is the proof, not the message.** Colonist roster read immediately before and immediately after the call:\n\n| | 301 Siqueira | 304 Maskinnen | 307 Simpson |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| before | up, no mental state | up, no mental state | up, no mental state |\n| after | up, no mental state | up, no mental state | up, no mental state |\n\nIdentical. On `20260901T195344` that call downed all three and started a berserk while returning `ok:true`, and four of them landed before it was noticed.\n\n## The general form on both kinds of verb\n\n`digest --wibble 3` returns `ok:true` and publishes `ignored_args` naming `wibble`, listing what `digest` actually read on that call (`colonists_cap`, `since`), and saying what the consequence is. `dev:spawn-thing --def WoodLog --pos … --mode direct --wibble 3` does the same and **additionally carries the journal seqs it wrote**, so \"what did that dropped argument cost me\" is answerable from the envelope alone — which is the exact complaint in comment #7, that the destructive call returned a truthful echo nobody read.\n\nThe `read` list is the caller's fastest route to the right spelling and it exists for all 120 verbs with **no declaration anywhere**.\n\n## The controls, because a refusal only means something against them\n\n- A CLEAN `digest` publishes **no** `ignored_args` key at all.\n- `journal-selftest --steps message --steps letter` still runs and returns `executed: [\"message\",\"letter\"]`. The guard refuses a stray key, not a legitimate call.\n- Session 15's near-miss still fires: `dev:spawn-thing --at` is refused with \"did you mean 'pos'?\".\n\n## Bullet 1 as literally written: STILL NOT MET, and I am saying so in those words\n\n`digest --wibble 3` SUCCEEDS. The general case reports, it does not refuse. The worker measured the literal form and it costs **73 keys across 26 verbs** that are read only on some paths while the verb still succeeds — `zone {op:\"add\", plant, dry_run:true}` where the preflight skips the only reader of `plant`; `dev:spawn-thing {stockpile, pos}` where `pos` goes unread when storage accepts; `queue` after the per-pawn gates in twelve verbs, which `accept/4087644-order-honesty.py` exercises on purpose; and `bill-add` answering `NotAWorkTable` before `ValidateBillArgs` reads its twenty levers. Refusing those would refuse legitimate calls mid-run on a live bench, which is worse than the defect.\n\n**What I am asking to be decided, and it is a scope call rather than work:** whether bullet 1 gets reworded to the report form, or stays an open goal now that the number behind it is 73/26. The dangerous half is closed either way — the harm in this issue's title was never \"an unknown key is ignored\", it was \"an unknown key is ignored, a default fires, and success is reported\", and the three verbs whose default MUTATES (`journal-selftest`, `pawn-fixture`, `world-fixture`) now refuse before acting.\n\n## What is genuinely new since comment #6\n\nComment #6 ran the bench and found `--wibble` accepted silently by eight verbs. That is no longer true of any verb: the call still succeeds, but it is never silent. The measurement that was static in comment #3 is now dynamic, and the read log is the thing that made it cheap — it follows the `VerbArgs` OBJECT, so the 22 forwarding handlers and the five runtime-built dicts stop being special cases. `trade-set` is the proof by construction: it reads its keys through a `foreach` over a string array, which no static scan can see and the read log gets for free.","files":null}]}